index 000000000..a03ca0994
--- /dev/null
+++ b/llvm/lib/Target/Eclipse/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,299 @@
+# The Eclipse LLVM backend
+
+An out-of-tree-style LLVM target (`lib/Target/Eclipse`, built via
+`LLVM_EXPERIMENTAL_TARGETS_TO_BUILD=Eclipse`) for the Data General
+Nova/Eclipse minicomputer family — a 16-bit, 4-accumulator, no-immediate-
+operand ISA from the 1970s. Structurally modeled on `lib/Target/Lanai`
+(LLVM's own recommended template for bringing up a new backend).
+
+This backend's ISA knowledge (register roles, addressing idioms, the
+calling convention, the `MUL`/`DIV` multiply-accumulate/divide
+convention, `dgasm`'s real assembly grammar) comes from a sibling project,
+`eccc` — a hand-written Rust C compiler targeting the same machine — whose
+`docs/ECLIPSE_ISA_NOTES.md` and `docs/CALLING_CONVENTION.md` verified every
+non-obvious fact by actually running it on `eclipseemu` (a real Data
+General Eclipse simulator) rather than trusting memory of the historical
+architecture. Where this backend goes beyond what eccc verified (i32,
+floats, structs), that's called out explicitly below rather than presented
+as equally solid.
+
+## Output format
+
+This backend emits **assembly text**, not object code: real Nova/
+Eclipse-S140 source for Connor Wood's
+[`dgasm`](https://github.com/CWood1/dgasm) cross-assembler — the same
+dialect eccc's own `SymbolicBackend`/`--dgasm` flag produces. There is no
+`MCCodeEmitter`/`MCAsmBackend`/`ELFObjectWriter`; `llc -filetype=obj` is
+not supported, only `-filetype=asm`. Assemble the result with:
+
+```
+dgasm -t eclipse_s140 -f simh -o out.simh out.s
+```
+
+and run it the same way eccc's own README documents (`eclipseemu` under
+WSL's `simh` package, or real hardware via `dgasm -f eclipse`).
+
+## Design decisions
+
+- **Registers**: AC0-AC3, matching real hardware. AC2 is permanently the
+  frame pointer, AC3 permanently holds `JSR`'s return address (both
+  reserved, unallocatable) — so only AC0/AC1 are available to the register
+  allocator. This mirrors eccc's own conclusion that "there was never a
+  register allocator worth building" for a 4-accumulator machine, though
+  this backend *does* run LLVM's real allocator over the remaining two,
+  spilling to frame slots under pressure rather than eccc's simpler
+  everything-is-a-memory-temp approach.
+- **i16 is the only legal type.** No 8-bit byte-addressing exists on this
+  ISA; every C type narrower than `int` promotes to i16 (mirrors eccc's
+  "char is one 16-bit word" simplification). i32 and all floating-point
+  types get no register class at all, so LLVM's generic type legalizer
+  handles them on its own: i32 `add`/`sub`/bitwise/shift/compare decompose
+  automatically into i16 pairs (its default behavior for an
+  oversized-but-unregistered integer type), and i32 `mul`/`div`/`rem` plus
+  every float operation fall back to runtime libcalls (`__muldi3`-style).
+  **Those runtime routines don't exist yet** — see "Known limitations."
+- **No immediate-operand instructions exist on the base ISA at all** (real
+  hardware backs this up: Nova/Eclipse never had an ADDI-style
+  instruction either), and this backend doesn't use the genuine extended
+  immediate instruction real Eclipse *does* have (`ADDI`) — every literal
+  constant this backend needs still goes through a `var`/constant-pool
+  data word, loaded with `LDA`. See `EclipseInstrInfo.td`'s file header
+  and `EclipseRegisterInfo::eliminateFrameIndex`'s `LEAFI` handling. What
+  real Eclipse *does* have, and this backend *does* use, is a genuine
+  extended-**addressing** instruction family (`EJSR`/`ELEF`/`ELDA`/
+  `ESTA`) — a 2-word encoding whose second word holds a real address
+  reaching the entire 32K-word address space directly, confirmed against
+  the real S/140 Programmer's Reference and empirically on eclipseemu
+  (see DEBUGGING_NOTES.md). That's an addressing-range capability, not an
+  arbitrary-immediate-constant one — it's what lets calls and global
+  access below skip page-zero indirection entirely, but it doesn't change
+  the "every literal constant is a `var`/constant-pool word" story above.
+- **Comparisons/branches**: every one reduces to a subtraction plus one of
+  four skip-then-jump conditional branches (`MOV#`/`MOVL#` +
+  `SZR`/`SNR`/`SZC`/`SNC` + `JMP`) — the same technique
+  `docs/CALLING_CONVENTION.md` verified. Unlike eccc (which always emits
+  both the true and false arm as separate `JMP`s, since it does no block
+  layout), this backend uses the tighter skip-and-fall-through form and
+  implements real `analyzeBranch`/`insertBranch` so LLVM's branch-folding
+  and layout passes can reason about it normally.
+- **Direct calls go through `EJSR`**, real Eclipse extended addressing
+  (`EJSR $func,0`, explicit absolute mode — see the extended-addressing
+  note above), reaching the callee directly with no page-zero
+  indirection at all. This replaced an earlier page-zero jump-table
+  scheme (`var <callee>_SLOT = <callee>`, `JSR @<callee>_SLOT,0`, closer
+  to eccc's own design) that existed only because base-ISA `JSR`'s
+  signed displacement can't reach an arbitrarily distant function on real
+  hardware — `EJSR` has no such limit, so unlike the old scheme this
+  costs zero page-zero words per distinct callee (see
+  DEBUGGING_NOTES.md). *Indirect* calls (through a runtime-computed
+  function-pointer value) are unrelated and unchanged: `EJSR` still can't
+  take a register operand on real hardware, so those still go through
+  the shared page-zero `_scratch` word (`CALLIND`/`CALLIND_JSR`) — see
+  DEBUGGING_NOTES.md entry #17.
+- **The software stack** (`_SP`, a plain page-zero word manipulated with
+  `ISZ`/`DSZ` plus one level of indirect addressing) and the
+  prologue/epilogue sequence (push caller's FP and return address, adopt
+  the new top as FP, reserve locals+temps; every function, including
+  leaves, uses the same sequence) are a direct port of
+  `docs/CALLING_CONVENTION.md`'s verified design. The real hardware
+  `PSH`/`POP`/`SAVE`/`RTN` stack instructions are not used — eccc found
+  they expect an OS-managed frame environment this bare-metal target
+  doesn't have. `LowerCall` (`EclipseISelLowering.cpp`) passes `0`, not
+  the argument count, as `CALLSEQ_START`'s reservation size — `PUSH`
+  (`emitPushPop`) already reserves its own word per argument via its own
+  `DSZABS` before storing, so reserving the same count again via
+  `ADJCALLSTACKDOWN` double-decremented `_SP` per argument, a leak
+  `ADJCALLSTACKUP` never fully undid. Invisible in a single shallow call
+  (nothing after it depended on the exact depth), it compounded across
+  nested/looped calls — e.g. `puts()` calling `putchar()` once per
+  character — until it clobbered `AC2`/`AC3` (frame pointer / return
+  address) badly enough to silently skip code or return through a
+  garbage address. Found via `eclipse_rt.c`'s `puts()`, which was the
+  first code in this project with that call shape.
+
+## Working end to end
+
+This has been built against a real llvm-project checkout and verified
+against real `dgasm` + `eclipseemu` runs (not just `.ll` hand-written
+tests) for all of the following:
+
+- **Full C frontend**: `clang -cc1 -triple eclipse-dg-none` works —
+  `clang/lib/Basic/Targets/Eclipse.{h,cpp}` and
+  `clang/lib/CodeGen/Targets/Eclipse.cpp` register a real `TargetInfo`/
+  `TargetCodeGenInfo`, so this is not "backend only, hand-write your own
+  `.ll`" — ordinary C source compiles.
+- **`char`/`i8`** loads, stores, and sign-extension (the ABI's
+  signext-return convention) — words are the only addressable unit on
+  this ISA, so `char` is a full 16-bit word underneath, but the C-visible
+  8-bit truncation/extension semantics are real (`LowerSIGN_EXTEND_INREG`
+  in `EclipseISelLowering.cpp`).
+- **`<<`/`>>`**, including arithmetic right-shift of negative values —
+  constant shift counts only (no variable-count shift/rotate-with-
+  writeback instruction is exposed). `<<` and the common multiple-of-4
+  case of unsigned `>>` use real Eclipse S/140 hardware, `HXL`/`HXR`
+  ("hex shift left/right" — one instruction shifts by N hex digits, i.e.
+  4*N bits, N in 1-4; verified against eclipseemu for every N, see
+  `EclipseInstrInfo.td`), which this backend didn't use at all until
+  now: e.g. `x << 15` used to cost 15 chained self-adds, now costs one
+  `HXL` plus 3 self-adds for the 3-bit remainder, and `x >> 8`/`x >> 4`
+  drop from the full multi-instruction hardware-`DIV` sequence
+  (`UDIVrr`'s post-RA expansion) to a single `HXR`. A non-multiple-of-4
+  `>>`, and every arithmetic `>>` (`HXL`/`HXR` are logical/zero-fill,
+  not sign-preserving), still go through the previous UDIV/SDIV-with-
+  floor-correction sequence (`LowerShift`). `DHXL`/`DHXR` (the same
+  shift on the 32-bit accumulator pair `AC(ac):AC(ac+1)`) are defined
+  and verified too but not yet wired into any codegen path — see
+  `LowerShift`'s comment for why (no register-pair class exists here,
+  and the whole register file is only two scratch accumulators).
+- **Signed and unsigned `*`/`/`/`%`.** `MUL`/`DIV` are zero-operand
+  hardware instructions operating on `AC0:AC1:AC2`; expansion happens
+  *post*-register-allocation (`EclipseInstrInfo::expandPostRAPseudo`), not
+  via the usual pre-RA custom inserter, because `AC2` doubles as the frame
+  pointer and the expansion has to be atomic with respect to spill-code
+  insertion — see the comment there for the corruption bug this fixes.
+  Signed division decomposes into abs-value + unsigned division +
+  conditional negate (`LowerSDIVREM`) rather than sign-priming the
+  hardware divide, since that only works for the unsigned case.
+- **Address-of-global** (`&global`, string literals used as `char *`,
+  array-to-pointer decay) via `LEAGA` in `EclipseInstrInfo.td`: expands to
+  a single `ELEF` (real Eclipse "extended load effective address")
+  instruction, which loads the operand's own resolved address directly —
+  confirmed empirically that `ELEF` returns the address, not the word
+  stored there (unlike `ELDA`). No page-zero pointer word involved at
+  all (see "Page-zero holds only what real extended addressing can't
+  reach" below). A *direct* load/store of a global's content (no
+  runtime-computed offset) instead goes straight through `ELDAGA`/
+  `ESTAGA` (real mnemonics `ELDA`/`ESTA`), one instruction in place of
+  `LEAGA` plus a `LDIND`/`STIND` dereference; every other case (a
+  runtime array index, an 8-bit extending/truncating access) still
+  composes `LEAGA`'s materialized address with `LDIND`/`STIND` exactly
+  as before. Address-of-*local* (`&x` for a stack variable) uses the
+  separate `LEAFI` pseudo — unrelated, still frame-index-based.
+- **Device I/O via inline asm** (`asm volatile("DOAS %0,011" :: "r"(c))`)
+  — the escape hatch for `SKPDN`/`SKPBN`/`DIAC`/`DOAS`/`NIO*`, which have
+  no ordinary-C mapping. `eclipse-toolchain/rt/eclipse_rt.c` wraps the
+  verified idiom (docs/IO_DEVICES.md in the sibling eccc project) as real
+  `putchar`/`getchar`/`puts`/`printf`/`scanf`, so most programs never need
+  to write inline asm themselves — `eclipse-toolchain/eclipse-cc` links it
+  in automatically. All confirmed against a real running `eclipseemu`,
+  including `scanf`/`getchar` with genuine live keyboard input (needs a
+  real pty in raw mode with `go`, not `step` — a piped/batch harness or a
+  canonical-mode pty can't deliver a keystroke while the simulator is
+  actually executing, only once it's back at the idle `sim>` prompt,
+  where it's misread as a command instead).
+- **Multi-file programs.** dgasm has no linker, so
+  `eclipse-toolchain/eclipse-cc` compiles each source (plus the runtime
+  library) to `.ll` separately and merges them with `llvm-link` before a
+  single `llc` run — the normal way to get a program made of more than
+  one `.c` file.
+- **Page-zero placement.** `LDA`/`STA ac,addr` default to page-zero
+  (absolute, 8-bit displacement) addressing, and dgasm assigns addresses
+  strictly in textual source order — so `var` declarations that must
+  actually *be in page-zero* (the constant pool, plus any long-jump
+  relaxation slots `reorder_asm.py` itself inserts — see below) have to
+  precede all code in the final file, regardless of which function
+  emitted them, while a global's *actual content* (never page-zero-
+  resident at all now that both calls and global access reach their
+  targets via real extended addressing — see "Design decisions" above)
+  is moved to the very *end* instead, after all code.
+  `eclipse-toolchain/reorder_asm.py` does this as a pure text pass after
+  `llc` (also sanitizing LLVM's `.str`-style anonymous-constant names,
+  which dgasm's identifier grammar rejects, and selectively relaxing any
+  internal jump that ends up out of dgasm's 8-bit PC-relative range) —
+  deliberately *not* solved inside `AsmPrinter` itself; see
+  `EclipseAsmPrinter.h`'s header comment for why. Its own address model
+  has to know that `EJSR`/`ELEF`/`ELDA`/`ESTA` are genuine 2-word
+  instructions (unlike everything else this backend emits) or its
+  end-of-program address calculation silently under-counts — see
+  DEBUGGING_NOTES.md for a real regression this caused and fixed.
+
+## Known limitations
+
+Recorded here deliberately, the same way eccc's own `docs/LIMITATIONS.md`
+does, rather than silently leaving gaps for someone to rediscover:
+
+- **`float` (IEEE-754 single precision) has a real soft-float runtime now**
+  (`eclipse_rt.c`'s `__addsf3`/`__subsf3`/`__mulsf3`/`__divsf3`, all six
+  comparison libcalls, and int<->float conversions) — verified correct on
+  `eclipseemu` individually and in small combinations. `double` (f64)
+  still has no runtime implementation (would need genuine 64-bit integer
+  support this backend doesn't have — see the `__muldi3`/i64 bullet
+  below) and no `i32` (`__muldi3` etc.) runtime either — programs needing
+  those will fail to *link* (no linker in this pipeline — dgasm resolves
+  symbols within one source file), not silently miscompile. Combining
+  *many* substantial float operations in one program (e.g. all four
+  arithmetic ops plus all six comparisons at once) can exceed the shared
+  256-word page-zero budget (see the budget bullet below) even though
+  each operation is individually correct — this is a real capacity limit
+  of the real hardware, not a correctness bug.
+- **No hand-verified Eclipse FPU opcodes.** Deliberately: eccc's own docs
+  explicitly refused to guess unverified ISA facts, and this backend
+  follows the same rule rather than fabricating `FAD`/`FSUB`/etc.
+  encodings from general historical knowledge of the Nova/Eclipse FPU
+  option. Wiring up real hardware float support (as an alternative to the
+  soft-float path above) is future work requiring the same
+  `eclipseemu`-verification methodology as `ECLIPSE_ISA_NOTES.md`.
+- **Variable shift counts are not supported** (`LowerShift` handles
+  constant counts only — `report_fatal_error`s otherwise). The real
+  `HXL`/`HXR`/`DHXL`/`DHXR` hardware shift instructions this backend now
+  uses for constant `<<`/multiple-of-4 `>>` (see the `<<`/`>>` bullet
+  above) don't change this: their own shift-count operand is a hardware
+  immediate (1-4), not a register, so they can't lower a runtime-
+  variable count either. `eclipse_rt.c`'s soft-float mantissa
+  alignment/normalization (`sf_shr`/`sf_shl`) is the main place this
+  would matter — it does a *runtime*-variable-count shift one
+  compile-time-constant-amount bit at a time in a C loop specifically to
+  work around this gap (see that function's own comment) — investigated
+  as part of the `HXL`/`HXR` work but not changed: each loop iteration's
+  individual shift is already the cheap single-bit case, so the loop's
+  real cost is per-iteration overhead (compare/branch/decrement), not
+  the shift instruction itself, and wiring `DHXL`/`DHXR` into it directly
+  would need inline asm against hardcoded physical registers (no
+  register-pair class exists, and the pair would consume the *entire*
+  two-register scratch file, leaving nothing for the surrounding
+  computation) — a real, separate, riskier change to an already fragile
+  file, not attempted here.
+- **No tail calls, single-word return values only.** (Varargs *are*
+  supported — real `va_start`/`va_arg`/`va_end`, which is what
+  `printf`/`scanf` are built on.)
+- **Not relocatable.** Globals/constants/functions all live at
+  compiler-chosen absolute addresses, baked in at compile time —
+  fundamentally position-*dependent*, same as eccc. `org 050` in
+  `EclipseAsmPrinter::emitStartOfAsmFile` is just a convenient default
+  address for paper-tape/console loading, not a relocation base; making
+  the output truly relocatable would mean reworking global/constant
+  addressing to go through a runtime-computed base instead.
+- **Page-zero holds only what real extended addressing can't reach —
+  fixed twice from the same underlying budget problem, most recently by
+  using real hardware capability instead of another layer of
+  indirection.** `LDA`/`STA ac,addr` only has an 8-bit displacement
+  (page-zero, 0-255 words), and this backend originally stored every
+  global's/string's *actual content* there directly, meaning the whole
+  program's data shared one 256-word budget — confirmed to fail outright
+  on nothing fancier than `printf` + `string.h` + `ctype.h` + `stdlib.h`
+  together in one file. First fixed by routing every global access
+  through one level of page-zero pointer indirection (`LEAGA` loading a
+  `var <name>_PTR = <name>` word, `LDIND`/`STIND` dereferencing through
+  it) — real progress (one word per global regardless of size, instead of
+  one word per word of content), but still a page-zero cost per distinct
+  global. Fixed again, more thoroughly, once real Eclipse extended
+  addressing (`ELEF`/`ELDA`/`ESTA` — confirmed against the real S/140
+  Programmer's Reference and empirically on eclipseemu; see
+  DEBUGGING_NOTES.md) turned out to reach the entire 32K-word address
+  space directly, with no page-zero involvement needed at all: a
+  global's address or content is now materialized/accessed with a
+  *single* instruction and *zero* page-zero words, full stop — see
+  "Design decisions" above (`LEAGA`) and "Working end to end" above
+  (`ELDAGA`/`ESTAGA`). The previously-failing `printf`+`string.h`+
+  `ctype.h`+`stdlib.h` combination compiles, assembles, and runs
+  correctly (verified, both before and after this second fix).
+  `eclipse-cc`'s `opt -passes=internalize,globaldce` step (strips unused
+  runtime-library functions before `llc`) remains in the pipeline as a
+  complementary, independent optimization. Constant-pool entries
+  (`tconstpool`) deliberately still use direct page-zero addressing, not
+  extended addressing — they're already exactly one word each, so there's
+  no page-zero cost to eliminate there in the first place. Calls went
+  through the same two-stage fix, for the same reason: a page-zero
+  `<callee>_SLOT` jump table (one word per distinct callee) first, then
+  direct `EJSR` (zero words) once extended addressing was confirmed —
+  see "Design decisions" above.
